        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Snow removal gets easier the moment you stop treating it as one job. A pusher and a scoop move the snow, an ice chopper deals with what has frozen, the right ice melt spread evenly stops it coming back, and a brush and compact shovel handle the car. That kit covers most winter mornings for most homes, and it costs less than any machine.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Add power equipment only when your driveway, your slope or your storms call for it: a power shovel for steps and short paths, a single-stage blower for a typical paved driveway, and a two-stage, ideally self-propelled, machine for long, steep, gravel or plow-bermed drives. Sort it all out in the fall, store it where you will reach for it, and the first storm becomes routine rather than a scramble.
          </p>
        </div>

